      "year": 2022,
      "abstract": "Abstract Immanuel Kant is often interpreted as a universal egalitarian who claims that all people, regardless of their differences, are equal. This view has been challenged by several scholars including Charles Mills and Robert Bernasconi, who note the persistent racist underpinning in Kant\u2019s work; however, the standard reading is that Kant changed his mind about race and eventually reaffirmed his universalism. By considering Charles Mills\u2019 notion of \u2018Black Radical Kantianism\u2019, as a way of reinventing Kant, I argue that continued engagement with Kant\u2019s universalism must go hand in hand with recognizing his racism in order to make it truly inclusive.",
